INT. FERRY TERMINAL LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

A concrete room beside the ticket hall. Twelve washers, eight dryers, a vending machine for soap. Fluorescent tubes buzz. Through a salt-stained window, the last ferry sits at its slip like a lit apartment block.

NADIRA HAQ, 30, in a rain jacket, hair braided back, holds a basket against her hip. She is staring at Dryer Six. In Dryer Six, a pair of red socks goes around and around.

On a plastic bench near the door sits ELLIS "BIRDIE" PAYNE, 28, Nadira's younger sister, in a big sweater, headphones around her neck. She watches Nadira like someone watching a pot.

At the folding table stands GUS TREMBLAY, 33, a sailor off a freighter in a navy peacoat, folding a single white shirt into thirds. He has a duffel at his feet. He does not know the sisters. He knows only that they are loud in the quiet way.

At the counter, behind sliding glass, MR. FITCH, 70s, the attendant, reads a newspaper he has read before.

BIRDIE
You're staring at them.

NADIRA
I'm not.

BIRDIE
Nadi. They're his socks.

NADIRA
They're not his. They're mine. They just look like his.

BIRDIE
You bought red socks after he left.

NADIRA
Not because of him.

BIRDIE
Because of the color red?

Nadira finally turns. She sets the basket on the table next to Gus, who slides his shirt to the side without comment. She starts pulling out towels, aggressively.

NADIRA
I said I'd be fine, and I am.

BIRDIE
You flooded the bathroom.

NADIRA
That was the tap.

BIRDIE
You were holding a mug at the time.

Gus coughs, politely. Nadira glances at him, then back at her sister.

NADIRA
Can we do this at home?

BIRDIE
We're not at home. We're at the ferry terminal because you wouldn't let me drive you.

NADIRA
I wanted to be on the water.

BIRDIE
You wanted to be on the water and instead you're in a laundromat.

Nadira stops. She holds a towel in both hands. The ferry horn sounds outside, one long note. Mr. Fitch turns a page.

NADIRA
He said he'd be on that one.

Birdie stands. Takes off her headphones. Walks over, slow, like approaching a horse.

BIRDIE
Nadi.

NADIRA
He said Thursday. The ten forty. He said, "I'll see you on the ten forty."

BIRDIE
Okay.

NADIRA
And I thought, if I'm already here, I'll just see him get off. And then I can decide.

BIRDIE
Decide what?

NADIRA
(sharp)
If I'm angry!

It rings in the little room. Gus becomes interested in a button. Mr. Fitch lowers the paper by an inch.

NADIRA (CONT'D)
(quieter)
I don't know what I am.

BIRDIE
You're soggy, mostly.

Nadira makes a noise that is half laugh and half something else. She drops the towel onto the table and presses the heels of her hands to her eyes. Birdie puts both arms around her from behind. The red socks go around and around.

GUS
Excuse me. Ma'am.

Both sisters turn.

GUS (CONT'D)
The ten forty is cancelled. Engine trouble. They told us on the dock.

NADIRA
Cancelled?

GUS
The next one's seven in the morning.

NADIRA
So he's not on it.

GUS
I don't know who "he" is. But nobody's on it.

Silence. Nadira looks at Birdie. Birdie looks at Nadira. Then Nadira begins to laugh, properly, helplessly, and sits down on the bench with her hand over her mouth, shoulders shaking. Birdie sits next to her.

BIRDIE
Oh my God.

NADIRA
He's not even coming.

BIRDIE
Nobody's coming, Nadi.

NADIRA
I could have stayed home!

BIRDIE
You could have stayed home.

They lean into each other, laughing, wiping their eyes. Gus returns to his shirt, folds the last third, and tucks it into the duffel. A small smile.

MR. FITCH
(not looking up)
Dryer Six is done, by the way.

NADIRA
Thanks, Mr. Fitch.

MR. FITCH
Take the socks out. They'll shrink.

She stands. Crosses to Dryer Six. Opens the door and the warm air rolls out over her face. She takes out the red socks, holds them up, looks at them for a long moment.

NADIRA
They're just socks.

BIRDIE
They're great socks.

NADIRA
They are great socks.

Nadira pairs them. Slips them into her basket. Birdie slings her headphones back around her neck. Gus lifts his duffel.

GUS
Good luck, ladies.

BIRDIE
You too, sailor.

He nods at them, at Mr. Fitch, and goes out into the wind. The door sighs shut. Nadira lifts the basket, and Birdie takes the other handle without being asked.

NADIRA
Seven in the morning.

BIRDIE
I'll make you eggs first.

They carry the basket out together. Mr. Fitch watches them go, then turns the page.
